<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02426</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.6.2: Choosing the Best Method to Solve an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>310002fb-a2ae-4f1c-ae5e-2c23dd0c91fd</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Write down your answer for how to solve each equation. Find the two solutions by rewriting it in factored form or by <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">completing the square</term>. Be prepared to explain your choice of method.</para>
      <exercise id="_0f5f4561-b47a-455e-8062-8d0aba4397b0" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(x^2−2x−1.25=0\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Correct! 2.5, -0.5</para>
          <para id="para-00004">The correct answers are: 2.5, -0.5.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Completing the square method:</para>
          <para id="para-00008">\(\begin{array}{rcl} x^{2}-2 x-1.25 &amp;=&amp;0 \\ x^{2}-2 x &amp;=&amp;1.25 \\ x^{2}-2 x+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;1.25+\left(\frac{-2}{2}\right)^{2} \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;1.25+1 \\ x^{2}-2 x+1 &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\ (x-1)^{2} &amp;=&amp;2.25 \\ \sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{2.25} \\ x-1 &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{2.25} \\ x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm \sqrt{2.25} \\ x &amp;=&amp;1 \pm 1.5 \\ x &amp;=&amp;1+1.5 \\ x &amp;=&amp;1-1.5 \\ x &amp;=&amp;2.5 \\ x &amp;=&amp;-0.5 \\ x&amp;=&amp;2.5 \text { and } x=-0.5 \end{array}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_60245351-60e4-44c6-982f-dba2c5c5710a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(5x^2+9x−44=0\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Correct! 2.2, -4</para>
          <para id="para-00011">The correct answers are: 2.2, -4.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00012">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00013">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Completing the square method:</para>
          <para id="para-00015">\(\begin{array}{rcl} 5 x^{2}+9 x-44 &amp;=&amp;0 \\ 5 x^{2}+9 x &amp;=&amp;44 \\ \frac{5 x^{2}+9 x}{5} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5} \\ x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\left(\frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{9}{5}\right)^{2} \\ x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{44}{5}+\frac{81}{100} \\ x^{2}+\frac{9}{5} x+\frac{81}{100} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\ \left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;\frac{961}{100} \\ \sqrt{\left(x+\frac{9}{10}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{\frac{961}{100}} \\ x+\frac{9}{10} &amp;=&amp;\pm \frac{31}{10} \\ x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10} \pm \frac{31}{10} \\ x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}+\frac{31}{10} \\ x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{9}{10}-\frac{31}{10} \\ x &amp;=&amp;\frac{22}{10}=2.2\\ x&amp;=&amp;-\frac{40}{10}=-4 \\ x&amp;=&amp;2.2 \text { and } x=-4 \end{array}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e9061560-631d-4849-8168-d06ba9152699" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00016"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(x^2+1.25x=0.375\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00017">Correct! 0.25, -1.5</para>
          <para id="para-00018">The correct answers are: 0.25, -1.5.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00019">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">Completing the square method:</para>
          <para id="para-00022">\(\begin{aligned} x^{2}+1.25 x &amp;=0.375 \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+((0.5)(1.25))^{2} &amp;=0.375+((0.5)(1.25)){ }^{2} \\ x^{2}+1.25 x+0.390625 &amp;=0.375+0.390625 \\ (x+0.625)^{2} &amp;=0.765625 \\ \sqrt{(x+0.625)^{2}} &amp;=\sqrt{0.765625} \\ x+0.625 &amp;=\pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625 \pm 0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625+0.875 \\ x &amp;=-0.625-0.875 \\ x &amp;=0.25 \\ x &amp;=-1.5 \\ x=0.25 &amp; \text { and } x=-1.5 \end{aligned}\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="f681bad2-7daa-463f-b9d9-36a177312aca" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00023"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions for \(4x^2−28x+29=0\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00024">Correct! \(\frac{7}{2}+ \sqrt {5}\), \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt {5}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00025">The correct answers are: \(\frac{7}{2}+ \sqrt {5}\), \(\frac{7}{2}- \sqrt {5}\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00008">
          <para id="para-00026">Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00008">
          <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Completing the square method:</para>
          <para id="para-00029">\(\begin{array}{rcl} 4 x^{2}-28 x+29 &amp;=&amp;0 \\ \frac{4 x^{2}-28 x+29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\ x^{2}-7 x+\frac{29}{4} &amp;=&amp;0 \\ x^{2}-7 x &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4} \\ x^{2}-7 x+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;-\frac{29}{4}+\left(\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} \\ x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;\frac{20}{4} \\ x^{2}-7 x+\frac{49}{4} &amp;=&amp;5 \\ \left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2} &amp;=&amp;5 \\ \sqrt{\left(x-\frac{7}{2}\right)^{2}} &amp;=&amp;\sqrt{5} \\ x-\frac{7}{2} &amp;=&amp;\pm \sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}\pm\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5} \\ x&amp;=&amp;\frac{7}{2}+\sqrt{5} \text { and } x=\frac{7}{2}-\sqrt{5} \end{array} \)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00030">Use completing the square to solve \(2x^2+10x+8=0\).</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      <section id="sect-00003">
        <title>Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square </title>
        <para id="para-00031">Solve \(2x^2+5x+2=0\) by <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">completing the square</term>.</para>
        <para id="para-00032"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. \(\frac{2x^2+5x+2}{2}=\frac{0}{2}\) \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+1=0\)</para>
        <para id="para-00033"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x=-1\)</para>
        <para id="para-00034"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((\frac{5}{2} \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{25}{16}\). \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-1+\frac{25}{16}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00035"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.   (Hint: create a common denominator before adding.) \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-\frac{16}{16}+\frac{25}{16}\) \((x+\frac{5}{4})^2=\frac{9}{16}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00036"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{5}{4})^2}= \sqrt{\frac{9}{16}}\) \(x+\frac{5}{4}= \pm \frac{3}{4}\)</para>
        <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. \(x+\frac{5}{4}=\frac{3}{4}\)         \(x+\frac{5}{4}=-\frac{3}{4}\) \(x=-\frac{2}{4}=-\frac{1}{2}\)   \(x=-\frac{8}{4}=-2\)</para>
        <note class="try" id="note-00002">
          <title>Try It: Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square</title>
        <exercise id="exer-00005">
            <problem id="prob-00009">
              <para id="para-00038">Solve \(4x^2+36x+37=0\).</para>
              <para id="para-00039">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
            </problem>
            <solution id="sol-00009">
              <para id="para-00040">Here is how to solve using completing the square:</para>
              <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. \(\frac{4x^2+36x+37}{4}=\frac{0}{4}\) \(x^2+9x+\frac{37}{4}=0\)</para>
              <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. \(x^2+9x=-\frac{37}{4}\)</para>
              <para id="para-00043"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((9 \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{81}{4}\). \(x^2+9x+\frac{81}{4}=-\frac{37}{4}+\frac{81}{4}\)</para>
              <para id="para-00044"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.   \((x+\frac{9}{2})^2=11\)</para>
              <para id="para-00045"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{9}{2})^2}=\sqrt{11}\) \((x+\frac{9}{2})= \pm \sqrt{11}\)</para>
              <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. \(x+\frac{9}{2}=\sqrt{11}\)       \(x+\frac{9}{2}=-\sqrt{11}\) \(x=-\frac{9}{2}+\sqrt{11}\)       \(x=-\frac{9}{2}-\sqrt{11}\)</para>
            </solution>
          </exercise></note>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
